{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And the silver of them that were numbered of the congregation was a hundred talents.</b> The silver of the half-shekel for each one of the sixty myriads of Israel came to a hundred talents, for six hundred thousand half-shekels are three hundred thousand whole shekels. And since Israel were counted in the wilderness of Sinai and came to six hundred thousand and three thousand and five hundred and fifty, therefore when there were in hand a hundred talents of half-shekels, there remained three thousand and five hundred and fifty, which are one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five whole shekels, which Moses left over. And Rashi wrote in his commentary: the talent is sixty maneh, and the sacred maneh was double, so the talent is one hundred and twenty maneh; and the maneh is twenty-five sela'im, so the sacred talent is three thousand shekels; and a hundred talents are three hundred thousand shekels — for six hundred thousand half-shekels are three hundred thousand whole. And the remainder, which was three thousand five hundred and fifty half-shekels, are one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five whole shekels. End of his words." "<b>And from this,</b> since a half-shekel for each one of the sixty myriads came to a hundred talents, a whole shekel for each one comes to two hundred talents — from here onward go and reckon with the shekels of Haman, which were ten thousand talents of silver that he intended to give on behalf of each one of the sixty myriads of Israel at the highest valuation in the Torah, which is fifty shekels, as written in the section of valuations. And when you reckon fifty shekels for each one of the sixty myriads — fifty times two hundred talents are a hundred times a hundred, and a hundred times a hundred are ten thousand talents. Or we may say that because Haman knew the sin of the tribes of Israel in the sale of Joseph for twenty pieces of silver, which are five shekels, and the punishment for that sin had not yet been exacted from them, therefore he devised a plan to acquire all Israel by giving on behalf of each one of the sixty myriads five shekels, for he thought that by this he would succeed and prevail through the magnitude of their sin. And as they said in the Midrash on (Genesis 37:25) ״וישבו לאכל לחם״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to them: You sold your brother in the midst of your feast; by your lives, your children will be sold in Shushan in the midst of a feast, as it is written (Esther 3:15) ״והמלך והמן ישבו לשתות״." "<b>Accordingly,</b> it would have been enough for him to give on their behalf a thousand talents of silver, for that is the reckoning of five shekels for each one of the sixty myriads, which comes to a thousand talents, according to the reckoning of a half-shekel for each one, which comes to a hundred talents." "<b>But</b> it is possible to say that he wished to include in this another intention: he wanted to take of the thousands a ten, and did so corresponding to the Ten Commandments, which he intended to overthrow with his ten. As they said (Esther 3:9) ״ועשרת אלפים ככר כסף אשקול״ — to nullify with his ten the merit of the Ten that were given from amidst fire and voice. And this is the language of אשקול — אש קול, 'fire, voice.' And the Holy One, blessed be He, in His mercy performed wonders with us, and His lovingkindness prevailed over us, such that even our handful of barley repulsed his ten thousand talents of silver; and there was turned upon his house the sound of barley — ״צליל שעורים״ — ״סערת י׳ חמה״, 'the storm of YHWH's heat,' to weaken his great power above, and his ten sons were slain below." "<b>And that which</b> it says, ״And the silver of them that were numbered of the congregation was a hundred talents,״ and likewise ״and the brass of the wave-offering was seventy talents,״ this indicates that Moses gave them a reckoning for each and every thing, and he delivered it all into the hand of Itamar, so that Israel would not suspect him when he was appointed sole treasurer over the Tabernacle. And they said in the Midrash about him: concerning him Scripture says (Proverbs 28:20) ״איש אמונות רב ברכות ואץ להעשיר לא ינקה״ — 'A faithful man shall abound with blessings, but he who hastens to be rich shall not be innocent.' איש אמונות — this is Moses, of whom it is said (Numbers 12:7) ״בכל ביתי נאמן הוא״. רב ברכות — for the Holy One, blessed be He, brought blessings through him, and he became treasurer over the work of the Tabernacle. ואץ להעשיר — this is Korah, who was a Levite and sought the high priesthood; therefore לא ינקה — what is written of him? (Numbers 16:32) ״ותפתח הארץ את פיה״. And just as a person must acquit himself of his obligation toward Heaven, so too must he acquit himself of his obligation toward the people, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, taught: the one who separates terumah must not enter wearing a lined garment, nor a shoe, nor a sandal, nor his money-pouch, nor his cloak, lest he become rich and they say, 'He grew rich from the terumah of the chamber,' or lest he become poor and they say, 'He grew poor from the chamber's funds.' And likewise a person must be careful that people not murmur and talk behind him, for so David prayed and said (II Samuel 22:44) ״ותפלטני מריבי עמי״, and the verse is complete (Numbers 32:22) ״והייתם נקיים מה׳ ומישראל״, and it is written (Proverbs 3:4) ״ומצא חן ושכל טוב בעיני אלהים ואדם״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:08:01.583655898Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}